commit a2cced01ea87d5b4cacd7ae2cc591a663cf5d2a5
Author: dev-194-fixes-6b <agent@farmtable.local>
Date:   Tue Jul 28 03:50:05 2026 +0000

    Make the lifecycle stage-set seam fail closed, and keep one copy of the rule
    (#194 round 6, B2 + B4 + B5; review F6, F7; audit A-3)
    
    B4 / F7 -- the fallback was fail-OPEN. store.LabelDeltaLifecycleStages required
    len(before) > 0 && len(after) > 0 and otherwise returned (current, current):
    "this label edit is not a lifecycle transition", which charges nothing. Every
    caller of these helpers is an authorization gate, so an implementer that
    returned an empty side bought a silently open gate. store.LifecycleStages had
    the same shape -- an empty set means the caller's charge loop runs zero times.
    
    Both now distinguish two cases that were conflated:
    
      - A store that does NOT implement LifecycleStageSetStager is answered from
        LifecycleStage. Unchanged, and still correct rather than a stub: a stage in
        its own column cannot be forged by a label.
      - A store that DOES implement it and returns empty has broken its contract,
        and now gets ErrEmptyLifecycleStageSet. Callers deny.
    
    The helpers therefore return an error, and UpdateTask's two gates propagate it
    as Internal rather than proceeding on a substituted answer.
    
    B2 / T-2 -- the rule was written twice. MultiStore repaired an empty inner
    answer itself, and ran first, so the store.go copy could never observe a
    violation. That is what round 5 measured: MUT_DELTA_FALLBACK and
    MUT_NATIVE_SPURIOUS survived with 0 failures on the store.go copy while the
    control MUT_MS_NATIVE_SPURIOUS on the MultiStore copy killed 1 test. MultiStore
    now propagates the inner store's answer verbatim, including an empty one, so
    the single remaining rule is the one that denies. Repairing it in MultiStore was
    also wrong on its own terms: MultiStore cannot return an error, so passing the
    violation up is the only way it can reach something that can refuse.
    
    B5 / F6 -- MultiStore now carries compile-time assertions for both
    LifecycleStager and LifecycleStageSetStager. It satisfied the set interface by
    accident; losing a method silently demoted every routed store to the
    one-element fallback with no compile error and no failing test.
    
    The equivalent assertion for GitHubPassThroughStore is NOT here: passthrough.go
    belongs to the parallel leg this round. Recorded for routing.
    
    New tests in internal/store/lifecycle_stage_set_test.go are tripwires against
    the observable contract, not against line numbers, and each negative row is
    paired with a positive control -- a healthy implementer must still be passed
    through untouched, or "it denied" would mean nothing.
    
    The fixture that makes this measurable is brokenStageSetStore: an implementer
    that returns empty sets. Round 5 could not express that input, which is why the
    fail-open branch went unmeasured for two rounds.

